{
	title: 'Shared work controls and progressive subagent help',
	spec: `Hal progressively discloses subagent orchestration and gives independently running commands and sessions shared coordination controls, while keeping their starting tools distinct.

Disclosure: when the remaining spawn budget is nonzero, the system prompt briefly says that subagents may be used, gives the recursive budget, and points to HELP SUBAGENTS. At zero, omit that section. Keep existing user-authorization policy. SPAWN and WAIT are absent from the default tools summary; SEND remains. HELP SUBAGENTS explains SPAWN kinds, fresh/fork modes, ownership, recursive budget accounting, automatic reports, questions and replies, WAIT, INSPECT, STOP and resuming. Direct HELP SPAWN, HELP WAIT and other tool help remain available; discovery is progressive, not access restriction. Use existing template bindings rather than requiring a new variable spelling.

Shared targets: STOP, WAIT and INSPECT accept a session ID such as 12-xyz or a tool-call reference #t123, t123 or 123. Bare numbers always mean tool calls in these controls, never tabs; tool references resolve within the calling session. Unknown, ambiguous or unsupported targets produce actionable errors. Keep existing INSPECT views usable. All capabilities remain behind the one native tool.

STOP ends the target execution and its owned work recursively, including running commands and owned subagents. Ownership, not SEND traffic or tab visibility, determines recursion. Stopping a tool call stops that operation and work it owns, including a background process whose initial tool result already returned. Preserve sessions, tabs, histories and partial output; report what stopped, what had already ended and any work that could not be stopped. Preserve safety validation and explicit unsafe-operation handling; do not claim cancellation rolls back external effects. No PAUSE tool. HELP SUBAGENTS explains that COMMAND /resume <session-id> reopens the selected stopped/closed session as needed, followed by SEND to start a new turn with preserved history. Already-open stopped sessions must have a usable continuation path. Interrupted tool calls and stopped descendants are not restarted automatically.

WAIT accepts one or more targets; without targets it waits for any owned background work, including agents and commands. It yields until completion, failure, cancellation or a question needs attention, not on ordinary streaming output. Events arriving before WAIT must not be lost. With no eligible work, return promptly with an explanation. Preserve full result/error delivery without duplicate reports or polling. INSPECT target provides status, available output and any pending agent question; it is a snapshot, not a required polling loop. SEND remains agent-only; background BASH and SPAWN remain separate starting operations.

Escape pauses the current turn and interrupts foreground tools under its existing rules, leaves the tab open, and leaves background commands and subagents running. This scope supersedes older blanket task wording that Escape stops every command. On Escape, show surviving background commands and owned subagents with actionable IDs and short descriptions, not only a count. Explain how to stop an individual job, all background jobs, or the delegated tree using the shared STOP facility through COMMAND where needed. Show this in both terminal and web; do not silently leave ongoing work undiscoverable. Preserve the rule that results arriving while the user has paused the session do not resume it.

/kill <id> remains selective. /kill all stops all background commands in the current session without stopping its agent or subagents, reports outcomes and retains output. Existing no-argument behavior remains.

Verification covers target resolution, recursive ownership boundaries, process cancellation and retained output, WAIT races and question delivery, Escape leaving background work running while revealing it, paused-result delivery, kill-all scope, and continuation without restarting descendants. Coordination is event-driven with bounded live bookkeeping, not repeated polling or unbounded event accumulation. Tab visibility is unchanged; bash tabs and hidden agents are a deferred follow-up.`,
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'Planning inspection: jobs.ts and tools/bash.ts deliberately detach background jobs from Escape cancellation; commands/kill.ts is the existing selective stop path. Earlier conversational claims that Escape already kills background jobs were wrong. Keep the final agreed Escape scope, reconciling older conflicting wording in xz, ker and 8v when implementing. /resume currently describes reopening closed sessions (18c); do not promise that an interrupted tool resumes. User-facing survivor notices need an actually callable route to recursive STOP, not merely instructions to type a model tool name as a slash command. Decide the smallest bridge during implementation. Optional tab visibility and process stdin messaging are deferred, not requirements of this task.'
